.Sh DESCRIPTION
Some engines are able to process multiple simultaneous crypto
operations.
This capability could be utilised to parallelise the processing of a
single connection.
For example a single write can be split into multiple records and each
one encrypted independently and in parallel.
Note: this will only work in TLS1.1+. There is no support in SSLv3,
TLSv1.0 or DTLS (any version).
This capability is known as "pipelining" within OpenSSL.
.Pp
In order to benefit from the pipelining capability.
You need to have an engine that provides ciphers that support this.
The OpenSSL "dasync" engine provides AES128-SHA based ciphers that have
this capability.
However, these are for development and test purposes only.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_max_send_fragment
and
.Fn SSL_set_max_send_fragment
set the
.Sy max_send_fragment
parameter for SSL_CTX and SSL objects respectively.
This value restricts the amount of plaintext bytes that will be sent in
any one SSL/TLS record.
By default its value is SSL3_RT_MAX_PLAIN_LENGTH (16384). These
functions will only accept a value in the range 512 -
SSL3_RT_MAX_PLAIN_LENGTH.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_max_pipelines
and
.Fn SSL_set_max_pipelines
set the maximum number of pipelines that will be used at any one time.
This value applies to both "read" pipelining and "write" pipelining.
By default only one pipeline will be used (i.e.
normal non-parallel operation).
The number of pipelines set must be in the range 1 - SSL_MAX_PIPELINES
(32). Setting this to a value > 1 will also automatically turn on
"read_ahead" (see
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_read_ahead 3 Ns ).
This is explained further below.
OpenSSL will only ever use more than one pipeline if a cipher suite is
negotiated that uses a pipeline capable cipher provided by an engine.
.Pp
Pipelining operates slightly differently for reading encrypted data
compared to writing encrypted data.
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_split_send_fragment
and
.Fn SSL_set_split_send_fragment
define how data is split up into pipelines when writing encrypted data.
The number of pipelines used will be determined by the amount of data
provided to the
.Xr SSL_write_ex 3
or
.Xr SSL_write 3
call divided by
.Sy split_send_fragment .
.Pp
For example if
.Sy split_send_fragment
is set to 2000 and
.Sy max_pipelines
is 4 then:
.Pp
SSL_write/SSL_write_ex called with 0-2000 bytes == 1 pipeline used
.Pp
SSL_write/SSL_write_ex called with 2001-4000 bytes == 2 pipelines used
.Pp
SSL_write/SSL_write_ex called with 4001-6000 bytes == 3 pipelines used
.Pp
SSL_write/SSL_write_ex called with 6001+ bytes == 4 pipelines used
.Pp
.Sy split_send_fragment
must always be less than or equal to
.Sy max_send_fragment .
By default it is set to be equal to
.Sy max_send_fragment .
This will mean that the same number of records will always be created as
would have been created in the non-parallel case, although the data will
be apportioned differently.
In the parallel case data will be spread equally between the pipelines.
.Pp
Read pipelining is controlled in a slightly different way than with
write pipelining.
While reading we are constrained by the number of records that the peer
(and the network) can provide to us in one go.
The more records we can get in one go the more opportunity we have to
parallelise the processing.
As noted above when setting
.Sy max_pipelines
to a value greater than one,
.Sy read_ahead
is automatically set.
The
.Sy read_ahead
parameter causes OpenSSL to attempt to read as much data into the read
buffer as the network can provide and will fit into the buffer.
Without this set data is read into the read buffer one record at a time.
The more data that can be read, the more opportunity there is for
parallelising the processing at the cost of increased memory overhead
per connection.
Setting
.Sy read_ahead
can impact the behaviour of the
.Xr SSL_pending 3
function (see
.Xr SSL_pending 3 Ns ).
In addition the default size of the internal read buffer is multiplied
by the number of pipelines available to ensure that we can read multiple
records in one go.
This can therefore have a significant impact on memory usage.
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_default_read_buffer_len
and
.Fn SSL_set_default_read_buffer_len
functions control the size of the read buffer that will be used.
The
.Fa len
parameter sets the size of the buffer.
The value will only be used if it is greater than the default that would
have been used anyway.
The normal default value depends on a number of factors but it will be
at least SSL3_RT_MAX_PLAIN_LENGTH + SSL3_RT_MAX_ENCRYPTED_OVERHEAD
(16704) bytes.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_tlsext_max_fragment_length
sets the default maximum fragment length negotiation mode via value
.Fa mode
to
.Fa ctx .
This setting affects only SSL instances created after this function is
called.
It affects the client-side as only its side may initiate this extension
use.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_set_tlsext_max_fragment_length
sets the maximum fragment length negotiation mode via value
.Fa mode
to
.Fa ssl .
This setting will be used during a handshake when extensions are
exchanged between client and server.
So it only affects SSL sessions created after this function is called.
It affects the client-side as only its side may initiate this extension
use.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_max_fragment_length
gets the maximum fragment length negotiated in
.Fa session .
.Pp
These functions cannot be used with QUIC SSL objects.
.Fn SSL_set_max_send_fragment ,
.Fn SSL_set_max_pipelines ,
.Fn SSL_set_split_send_fragment ,
.Fn SSL_set_default_read_buffer_len
and
.Fn SSL_set_tlsext_max_fragment_length
fail if called on a QUIC SSL object.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
All non-void functions return 1 on success and 0 on failure.
.Sh NOTES
The Maximum Fragment Length extension support is optional on the server
side.
If the server does not support this extension then
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_max_fragment_length
will return: TLSEXT_max_fragment_length_DISABLED.
.Pp
The following modes are available:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It TLSEXT_max_fragment_length_DISABLED
Disables Maximum Fragment Length Negotiation (default).
.It TLSEXT_max_fragment_length_512
Sets Maximum Fragment Length to 512 bytes.
.It TLSEXT_max_fragment_length_1024
Sets Maximum Fragment Length to 1024.
.It TLSEXT_max_fragment_length_2048
Sets Maximum Fragment Length to 2048.
.It TLSEXT_max_fragment_length_4096
Sets Maximum Fragment Length to 4096.
.El
.Pp
With the exception of
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_default_read_buffer_len
.Fn SSL_set_default_read_buffer_len ,
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_tlsext_max_fragment_length ,
.Fn SSL_set_tlsext_max_fragment_length
and
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_max_fragment_length
all these functions are implemented using macros.
